WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
The fact is this, 99% of people are

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:07.240
using AI the wrong way and even worse,

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.680
most people don't even know the

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.120
potential that they are missing out on.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.360
And so in this video, I'm going to show

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.240
you the way that power users like myself

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.920
use AI differently so that we can get

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.680
the very best outputs and the very best

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.960
results. And the first thing that the

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.720
top 1% do is they create custom AI tool

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.400
workflows [music] rather than just

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.840
getting ChatGPT to do everything. Yep,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.040
it turns out that if you use ChatGPT and

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.520
at least one other AI chatbot, then

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:38.280
congratulations, you are in the top 10%

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.080
of AI users already. Because in surveys,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.880
less than 10% of ChatGPT users use more

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:46.480
than one. But relying on ChatGPT to do

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:49.400
everything is a mistake. Since as these

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.080
benchmarks show, there are other AI LLM

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.960
chatbots that do some things much better

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.000
than ChatGPT. For example, one ChatGPT

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.520
competitor, Claude from a company called

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.640
Anthropic, is much much better at coding

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.120
and writing. And Google's ChatGPT

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.360
competitor, Gemini, has much better

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.640
content creation tools with its music,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.560
image, and video generation. Which is

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.120
why in my AI tutorials on this channel,

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.560
like this one here where I showed how to

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.920
create commercial AI novels, I crafted a

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.240
custom AI tool workflow that used the

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.800
very best tools to do each individual

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.400
task needed to create the very best

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.480
novel possible. And there is also

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.560
something else that the top 1% of users

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.240
do to get the best results. And that is

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:36.120
that they get AI to ask them questions.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.960
Yes, so anytime I meet an AI skeptic who

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.760
goes, "Oh, I tried to create something

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.920
with AI and it wasn't very good." I'm

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.640
always like, "Okay, did you only try

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.880
using the free version of ChatGPT to do

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.480
everything for you?" And usually the

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.920
answer to that is yes, so there's that.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.400
But secondly, I'll often say, "Okay, but

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.280
did you try it again? But this time, get

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:00.480
the AI to ask you questions before you

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.840
get it to generate something." Because

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.800
this is what the top 1% of users do. So,

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.640
for example, I opened up Claude and

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.520
asked it to make me a new business

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.640
website and I asked it to interview me

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.320
and ask me questions before it made it.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.520
And that's exactly what it did. It

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.600
created a series of interview questions

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:21.000
to ask me so that it could get the

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.440
context that it needed to code together

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.600
a great website for me that met my

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.560
expectations. This is particularly

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.320
useful for things that you aren't an

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.600
expert in. So, for me, I'm not an expert

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.320
in web design and development. So, I

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:37.040
didn't know how to prompt it to get me

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.040
the result I wanted. But luckily, I

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.920
didn't need to be. By answering Claude's

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.160
interview questions, I was able to give

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.360
it the context that it needed to create

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.920
an awesome website for me. Plus, there

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.880
is something else that top AI users do

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.640
to get the best results. And that is

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:54.800
that they break complex tasks down into

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.240
chunks. So, speaking of Claude, take

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.280
this video here. In this, I shocked a

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.040
lot of people by getting Claude to

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.880
create a cozy romance novel which was

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.600
surprisingly pretty good. But as I said,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.560
it surprised a lot of people. Because

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.600
most people who have tried this just

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.560
open up ChatGPT or Claude and go, "Hey,

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.840
I want to write a novel. Here is my

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.360
topic. Generate chapter one for me." And

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.680
then bam, that's it. So yeah, if you do

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.960
it this way, chances are what it creates

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:25.520
will be pretty underwhelming. But you

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.160
think about it, do most human authors

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.640
write a good book this way? No, they

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.000
break the process down into chunks. In

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.880
the writing world, this is called

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.640
plotting. They plan the ending, the

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.520
characters, and the storyline before

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.600
they write it. So, that's the process I

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.960
went through with AI too. First, I used

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.920
Claude to help me come up with the

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.960
ending ideas for my book. Then I used it

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.160
to identify characters romance novels

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.360
usually include and to create profiles

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.480
for them. And then after that, I asked

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.600
it to create a storyline for me by using

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.560
a Save the Cat storyline structure.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.920
Which if you don't know what that is,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.000
it's a classic 15-arc plot structure

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.360
that is popular with romance books. And

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.440
then, only then did I get it to write

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:07.360
each of the chapters for me. And this

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.920
made my output much much better. And

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:11.200
there is something else that top AI

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.080
users do that makes their outputs much

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.680
better. And that is that they don't just

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:18.079
accept the first thing that AI gives

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.600
them. And this is in my opinion what

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:24.520
separates high-quality AI creations from

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.640
low-quality AI slop. So recently in a

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.880
tutorial, I showcased a little clip I

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.200
made of a puppy and a kitten having a

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.720
podcast. And to do this, I used a

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.520
technique called frame to video where

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.120
you generate a picture and then you get

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.240
AI video generation tools to use it as

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.680
the starting frame and to animate the

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.880
rest of the video. And so, this is how

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.680
the video turned out once I tweaked the

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.920
voices a bit. What is your favorite

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.640
game?

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:53.440
Hmm, I'm not sure. I think I like fetch

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.400
the most. Super cute, right? But to get

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.000
my perfect starting frame, I had to

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:00.120
become a creative director and direct

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.760
the AI to make it. Because this was the

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.240
first image it actually generated for

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.240
me. Nowhere near as cute, right? So then

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.440
I got it to zoom in on them and change

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.720
the angle. Then I got it to remove the

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:13.080
laptop and I got it to give the kitten a

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.920
little mini microphone. Then I got rid

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.680
of the bigger microphone off on the

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.560
right. And then I gave the puppy

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.320
headphones. And that was it. I had my

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.000
perfect starting frame. So that's the

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.400
key to creating the best outputs with

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.320
AI. It's not just taking whatever it

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.680
gives you. It's when you step into the

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.240
role of creative director and treat AI

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:32.880
as a virtual employee who fulfills your

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.480
vision. Which actually brings me on to

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.520
the next thing that top 1% of users do,

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:39.480
which is they experiment and try new

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.720
prompts even if they don't know that

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.320
they will work. Seriously, I find on the

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:45.520
flip side that there are some people who

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.560
greatly underestimate AI. They think of

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.080
it of course as a virtual employee, but

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:52.040
only one that can do menial boring work.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.600
But you should experiment with it more

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:56.520
because sometimes it can really surprise

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.480
you. For example, in this video, I

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.200
showed how I discovered these kids learn

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.480
to draw books that people were selling

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.400
on Amazon. Basically, they are books

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.400
which take kid-friendly pictures and

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.600
then break them down into a step-by-step

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.840
process that children can follow to draw

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:12.680
them. Now, I had no idea if AI could

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.600
make these or not as I had never seen

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.760
anyone else try this before. But I

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:19.080
thought, let's give it a go, try it, and

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:21.200
find out. So I broke this complex task

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.240
down into steps. First, I uploaded a

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.360
reference image and I got it to draw for

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:27.800
me a kid-friendly goldfish in the same

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.680
style, which it did perfectly. And then,

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.560
the moment of truth. I asked it to take

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.120
this image and then to turn it into a

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.720
learn-to-draw style picture. And look,

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:39.240
it figured it out perfectly. I didn't

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.200
even need to explain what it was. And

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.480
so, I just made a few tweaks to it and

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.160
then boom, I had my own learn-to-draw

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:48.680
picture exactly like the popular books.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:51.440
So yeah, these days I always give

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.800
something a go with AI first because in

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.920
my experience, it can really surprise

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:58.160
you. And here is something else that top

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.800
1% users do. And that is that they will

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.760
minimize AI hallucinations by running

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.960
the same prompt through multiple AIs.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.680
So, when I talk to AI skeptics, one of

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.720
the most common complaints is that AI

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.160
hallucinates. As in, it will tell you

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.320
information that sounds plausible but

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.560
actually is not true. And yes, it is

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.400
super annoying since it means you never

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.880
quite know what it's telling you is true

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.640
or not. Which is exactly why the top 1%

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.520
of users run the same prompt through

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.440
multiple AIs to see if they get

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:28.800
different results. Because the way that

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:30.840
AI chatbot LLMs work is that when they

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.880
reply to you, data tokens are actually

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.480
behind the scenes predicting each word

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.640
to reply with based on statistical odds

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.000
from the data it has access to. So let's

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:42.000
imagine for example, you asked an AI,

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.240
"What is the capital city of Australia?"

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.880
Well, the correct answer is Canberra.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:48.840
But a lot of people do mistakenly think

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.480
that it's Sydney. So because the answer

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.280
of Sydney is mixed into the training

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.240
data for AIs, it means then that this

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.080
could potentially come up as an answer

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:59.360
for them because there is a chance the

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:02.680
token for an AI could accidentally

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:05.560
predict it. So if you only ask one AI

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.880
for an answer and it hallucinates, then

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.320
you have no way to know. Which is why

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:12.800
I'll often run the same prompt through

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:15.200
four AIs: ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity,

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.960
and Gemini. That way if all four give me

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:19.320
the same answer, then I know it has a

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:21.080
much higher chance of being correct.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.400
Plus, another way that top AI users

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:26.240
avoid hallucinations is they use a very

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.880
different AI tool, Notebook LM. So,

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.680
Notebook LM, if you've never heard of

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:32.120
it, is Google's AI study tool and

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.800
learning tool. And it is unique because

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:36.400
it is a source-grounded AI. And this is

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.280
very important. So let me show you an

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.159
example. Let's say you had just gotten

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.360
the graphics design app Canva, but you

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:43.039
were overwhelmed with all of the

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.120
buttons. And you wanted to be able to

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:47.360
ask an AI questions about how to use

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.800
Canva to do specific things. Well, what

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.680
you could do is you could come to

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.240
tutorial videos from YouTube creators

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:56.520
you trust, such as my Canva tutorial

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:58.640
video, and copy the video URLs. And then

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:01.400
you could open up Notebook LM and create

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:04.120
a new notebook and paste in the URLs for

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:06.800
these tutorial videos. And now, Notebook

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:09.000
LM will use these as trusted sources and

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:11.360
create your very own custom chatbot for

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.600
you inside of your notebook. And this

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.520
chatbot will only use the sources you

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.960
trust to answer any questions you give

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.640
it. This is why we call it a

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:22.000
source-grounded AI. Not only this, but

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:24.200
it also has to cite every answer it

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.360
gives back to the sources so that you

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.440
can verify them. And even better, if you

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:31.560
ask it a question which your sources

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.680
don't have an answer for, then it is

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.360
actually trained to tell you that it

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:39.360
doesn't know rather than just inventing

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.040
an answer that sounds plausible. And

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.200
here is another crucial thing that top

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.520
AI users do. And that is that they start

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.840
a new chat whenever they see that their

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:51.280
AI is stuck in a loop. So, have you ever

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.760
given AI a task to do and then it's come

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.280
back to you and said, "Hey, I've done

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:56.040
it." And you're like, "Great." And then

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:58.000
you check it and then you're like, "Wait

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:00.320
a minute. Nothing has changed. You

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.560
didn't do the task that I told you to

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.160
do. Well, if you have had that happen to

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.960
you, it is super annoying. And what it

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:10.320
generally means is that your AI is now

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.240
stuck in a loop. It happened to me a few

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.520
months ago. I was making a thumbnail for

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:16.440
YouTube, and I put the Nano Banana icon

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.600
on it. And I thought, you know, that

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:20.800
banana looks kind of boring. So, I

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.760
decided to upscale it and to bolden it

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.040
and to make it more exciting to look at

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:27.640
by using Google's logo colors for the

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.720
peel. So, I uploaded it into, surprise

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:32.480
surprise, Google's Nano Banana image

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:34.640
editor and broke this complex task down

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.920
into chunks, getting it to change one

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:38.240
thing each time. [music]

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:40.720
And this was very successful until I got

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:43.000
to the end and I said to it, "Hey, make

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.560
the line art thicker on this picture."

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.920
And it didn't do it. The line art stayed

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:50.320
the exact same thickness as before.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.400
Well, I immediately realized that my AI

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.440
was now stuck in a loop. The AI chat

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.680
context window had been accumulating

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.480
data throughout the conversation on what

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:00.800
the lines of my picture should look

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:02.920
like. So, when I asked it to redraw it

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:05.160
but thicker, the data from my previous

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:07.839
line art that it had been generating

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.200
polluted this output. So, the solution

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:11.360
was actually quite simple. I just

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.720
started a new chat and I gave it the

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.560
same request and [music] it did it.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:18.839
Easy. Problem solved. So, then onto a

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.120
trick I like to use, which is to use AI

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:24.360
to create new, interesting, original

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:26.440
content by targeting information gaps.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.920
So, one quirk of AI is that if you ask

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:30.720
it to create content for you, it will

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.120
create very unoriginal copycat content

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.280
by default. So, for example, I asked

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:37.320
ChatGPT to write me an article with five

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.920
tips on how to use AI effectively and it

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:41.120
wrote me an article with all of the

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.800
usual tips you'd expect, like be

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.480
specific, give context, things you've

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:47.320
likely heard before. And there are

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:49.360
several reasons why it did this, but one

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:51.560
big reason is that AI's a pattern

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:54.120
matching machines. They spot patterns in

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:55.880
pre-existing data and focus on them. So,

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.760
since these are the most common ideas

00:11:55.880 --> 00:12:00.160
that it sees in articles, it will

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:02.560
naturally converge on them by default.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.520
Now, for some tasks, this is actually

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.200
super helpful. Like you take that

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.600
romance novel I mentioned that I wrote

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:10.600
with AI earlier, I actually wanted that

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:13.160
to be familiar. I wanted to make it a

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:15.480
fun, comfort food style reading. So,

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:17.880
sometimes clichés can actually be quite

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.760
good. But sometimes that's not what you

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:22.400
want, right? Sometimes you want new,

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:24.760
original ideas and content. So, here's

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.720
what I did. First, I asked ChatGPT to do

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:29.720
a search and to find five things that

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:31.760
are annoying people about AI that they

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:33.800
don't think people are talking about or

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.600
addressing because that way, by talking

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:38.400
about topics that people want talked

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:40.320
about but that aren't being right now, I

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:43.200
can create content that is both new,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.400
original, and helpful. Then, once it had

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:48.040
identified those five grievances that

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:50.480
people are rightfully having about AI, I

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:51.960
got it to write an article about those.

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:53.800
And it was super interesting. Some of

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.800
the fixes that it came up with for

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:57.120
overcoming them are ideas that I've

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:59.200
actually already talked about in this

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.240
video, such as breaking down complex

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:03.360
tasks into chunks to get much better

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:05.960
results. And here is something else that

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:08.120
top users do to avoid another quirk of

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:10.800
AI, and that is that they avoid

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:13.160
sycophancy [music] by starting new chat

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:15.560
threads. So, it's been well documented

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:17.440
at this point, AI sycophancy is real.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:19.920
This is when AI continuously tells you

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:22.080
that your ideas are great, that you are

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:23.680
so smart, things like that. Now, some

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.760
people do like this and they find it

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:28.080
makes AI more friendly. And fair enough,

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.240
I get it. But a lot of people, like me,

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:32.520
do find it really annoying. It makes it

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:34.440
hard to use it for feedback since, well,

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.839
how can you trust it if it says that

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:38.600
your work is good? What if the AI is

00:13:36.839 --> 00:13:40.880
just flattering you? It's a fair

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:42.960
question. So, a useful tip to know is

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:44.839
that the longer a chat thread goes, the

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.120
more likely it is that the AI is going

00:13:44.839 --> 00:13:48.880
to turn into a sycophant. And that's

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.800
because of the fact that AI is trained

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:53.560
with a technique called RLHF,

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:55.760
which is an acronym for reinforcement

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:57.880
learning from human feedback. Basically,

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:00.360
AI learns to adapt its behavior based on

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:02.400
user satisfaction. So, you think about

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:04.400
it. The longer your chat thread goes,

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:06.280
the more AI can see, based on the way

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:09.160
that you're interacting with it, what

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:11.120
you like and what you don't like. In the

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:13.560
AI world, we call this a chat thread

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:16.040
context window. The more you chat with

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:18.640
AI, the more it leans into what you are

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:21.400
responding positively towards, and the

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:23.640
more likely it is to reinforce it. You

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:25.400
become stuck in an echo chamber. So,

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:27.320
that is why to get the most

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:29.760
matter-of-fact critique, I start a new

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:32.240
chat and turn on temporary chat mode,

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:34.440
otherwise known as incognito mode. This

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.320
means it can't access memories of chats

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:38.400
that you already have had with it. So,

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:41.240
it can't cheat by looking back at the

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:43.560
past of what I've liked and reinforced

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:45.560
before giving me an answer. Plus, there

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:47.839
is something else that the top 1% users

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:51.040
do, and that is that they save a lot of

00:14:47.839 --> 00:14:53.480
money by picking the right AI model. So,

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.240
yeah, the $20 a month pro AI plans for

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:57.600
the major AI tools are all a pretty

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:59.720
great value, but they do have token

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:01.320
usage limits. And if you want to upgrade

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:04.400
your usage limits, they can get quite

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:06.680
pricey. Like we're talking $100 a month

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:09.240
pricey or paying per usage, which is

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:11.240
often even more expensive at scale. So,

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:13.560
it's a good idea to try and save your

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:15.520
usage by picking the cheapest model for

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:17.600
the job you want it to do. So, you take

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:19.920
Claude. Right now, they have three main

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:21.839
models. Haiku is the least smart, but it

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.640
also uses the least computing power and

00:15:21.839 --> 00:15:25.320
therefore the least amount of tokens.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.880
Sonnet is their mid-tier. It's a lot

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:30.360
smarter than Haiku, so it uses more

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:32.120
computing power, but then you have Opus,

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:34.360
which is their smartest model that uses

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:36.079
by far the most computing power and

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:38.560
therefore means you hit your usage

00:15:36.079 --> 00:15:40.520
limits much faster. The thing though is

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.920
that sometimes more intelligence doesn't

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:46.280
necessarily equal a better result if the

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:48.079
task itself is not overly complex. So, I

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:50.360
opened up Claude code and turned on

00:15:48.079 --> 00:15:52.320
Sonnet and I asked it to code for me a

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:54.839
website that showcased my local time

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:57.120
zone, the New Zealand time zone, against

00:15:54.839 --> 00:15:58.880
other international time zones. And when

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:00.920
it was done, here is what Sonnet

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:02.880
created. Pretty cool, right? You can

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:05.079
come in and customize the site by

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:06.600
choosing your own time zones to track

00:16:05.079 --> 00:16:08.920
and everything.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:10.880
So, then, I now put Opus to the test,

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:13.640
their advanced model that eats up your

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:15.280
usage limits much faster than Sonnet.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:17.480
And so, I got it to make the same thing

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:20.240
for me. And when it was done, it created

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:22.760
this. Again, another great website, but

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:24.880
let's be honest, what Sonnet built was

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:27.640
just as good because, well, this was a

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:29.839
fairly simple task. So, even if Opus is

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.800
smarter technically than Sonnet, it's

00:16:29.839 --> 00:16:33.839
irrelevant in this case because Sonnet

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:35.079
is also smart enough to build this for

00:16:33.839 --> 00:16:36.680
me, too.

00:16:35.079 --> 00:16:38.600
So, then, if you'd like to learn about

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:40.720
even more tricks that you can utilize to

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:42.440
grow and scale businesses using AI

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:44.520
tools, be sure to watch my next video

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:47.320
here on screen and I will see you in the

